Strong consistency is like pausing an entire company meeting until every single person in three different time zones writes down the exact same number.
Eventual Consistency is telling the person next to you and letting the update ripple across the room—nobody has to stop working, and within a fraction
of a second, everyone naturally converges on the exact same truth.

Technical Breakdown :- 
Eventual Consistency is a consistency model used in distributed systems (like Apache Cassandra, Amazon DynamoDB, and global DNS) to 
guarantee high availability and low latency.

Instead of locking all replicas during a write and waiting for every node across the globe to confirm, the system accepts the write on one local node 
and immediately returns a 200 OK to the user. In the background, that update is asynchronously replicated to the other nodes.

The formal guarantee is simple: if no new updates are made to a given record, all replicas will eventually converge to the exact same 
value—typically within milliseconds. During that brief replication window, a user reading from a lagging replica might see slightly stale data.
Under the hood, systems enforce convergence using background mechanisms like Read Repair, Hinted Handoff, and Anti-Entropy (Merkle Trees).

Buisness Context :- 
For global, high-traffic applications, waiting for synchronous cross-continent agreement destroys user experience and revenue. 
If a shopper in Bengaluru adds an item to their cart, or a user likes a post, they do not care if a replica in Virginia sees 1,004 likes 
instead of 1,005 for 150 milliseconds—they do care if the button freezes for two seconds or fails during a network hiccup. Eventual consistency keeps
the storefront open and blazingly fast 24/7.

Design Decision :- 
Some engineers either fear eventual consistency—assuming it means permanent data corruption—or blindly use it for financial ledgers, 
causing catastrophic double-spending. As a data engineer, I match the consistency model to the business invariant: I use strong consistency 
for money and inventory counts, and eventual consistency for feeds, catalogs, and telemetry. Even better, when using eventually consistent stores,
I prevent jarring UX bugs by implementing Read-Your-Own-Writes (sticky sessions or version tokens) so a user never updates their profile and sees 
their old bio on refresh, and I leverage Quorum tuning (R + W > N) when a specific critical query demands strong consistency on top of an AP database.
